{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Feed me, I pray you, etc. Therefore was his name called Edom.</b> It is obvious that 'therefore' implies he called him Edom because of the feeding of the pottage of lentils, and it would have been more fitting to call him Edom because he came out red. Therefore it appears to explain the verse thus: ״הלעיטני נא מן תבשיל זה אדום לי״ — 'feed me, I pray you, from this stew, it is red to me' — שאני אדום, 'for I am red,' a play on words (לשון נופל על הלשון). על כן — because he called himself Edom, since he came out red, his name was called Edom." "<b>For I am faint.</b> For on that day he killed Nimrod, who had fought with him. For when Esau began to hunt in the field, Nimrod found him — he was a mighty hunter — and said to him that no man is permitted to hunt in the fields except himself, and he set a day for battle. Esau came and took counsel from Jacob, and he said to him: as long as Nimrod is wearing his coveted garments, you cannot prevail against him; but say to him that he should remove them, then you will be able to prevail against him. And so he did, and he killed Nimrod. And therefore he said 'for I am faint,' as it is written: ״כי עיפה נפשי להורגי״."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:06.157192543Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}